NAME
       devctl - ask the device manager (devd) about disks and USB drives

SYNOPSIS
       devctl list
       devctl monitor
       devctl fstypes
       devctl mount name
       devctl unmount name
       devctl eject disk
       devctl format name fs [--label label] [--full] [--cluster bytes]

DESCRIPTION
       devctl talks to devd(1), for testing it without a desktop.

       list      the disks and volumes: size, file system, label, whether
                 it is a USB drive, whether it belongs to the running system,
                 and where it is mounted.

       monitor   print every device event (ADDED, REMOVED, CHANGED) until
                 interrupted.

       fstypes   the file systems devd knows, which it can mount and which
                 it can format.

       mount, unmount
                 mount a volume (name as in devctl list, e.g. sdb1) at
                 /media/<user>/<label> and print the path; unmount it.

       eject     unmount every volume of a drive (e.g. sdb) and flush it, so
                 it can be pulled out.

       format    make a new file system (vfat or ext4) on a volume.  Quick
                 unless --full is given, which writes zeros over the whole
                 volume first.  --cluster sets the allocation unit size.
                 Everything on the volume is lost.

       mount, unmount, eject and format are allowed for root and for the
       user logged in at the console, on removable USB drives only.

EXIT STATUS
       0 on success, 1 when devd refused or failed (the reason is printed),
       2 for a bad argument.

SEE ALSO
       devd(1), lsblk(1), mount(1)

